Subject: [PATCH 4/7] decoder: accept finite integer-valued floats into int / uint

Pipelines that round-trip data through encoding/json lose the
integer / float distinction because encoding/json decodes every JSON
number into float64. Re-encoding such a map with msgpack and then
decoding into a struct whose field type is int64 or uint64 panicked
with

    msgpack: invalid code=cb decoding int64

even though the value (e.g. float64(10)) was a perfectly
representable integer. This made the v5 line unusable for a common
JSON-then-msgpack pattern.

This patch teaches Decoder.int and Decoder.uint to accept
msgpcode.Float and msgpcode.Double in addition to the integer codes
they already accepted. A float value is converted only when it is

  * finite (not NaN, not +/-Inf),
  * integer-valued (f == math.Trunc(f)),
  * representable in the target type's range.

Otherwise a clear error is returned, so silently truncating data
remains impossible. Strict pipelines that send true integer codes are
unaffected.

Backwards compatible: previously errored input now decodes; previously
successful input is unchanged.

Apply from the package root:

    patch -p1 < 0004-decoder-accept-float-into-int.patch

---
 decode_number.go | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

--- a/decode_number.go
+++ b/decode_number.go
@@ -115,6 +115,21 @@
 		return uint64(n), err
 	case msgpcode.Uint64, msgpcode.Int64:
 		return d.uint64()
+	case msgpcode.Float, msgpcode.Double:
+		// Accept floats produced by pipelines that lose the integer /
+		// float distinction (encoding/json decodes JSON numbers into
+		// float64). Conversion succeeds only when the value is finite,
+		// integer-valued, and fits in uint64; anything else is rejected
+		// so meaningful data cannot be silently truncated.
+		f, err := d.float64(c)
+		if err != nil {
+			return 0, err
+		}
+		if math.IsNaN(f) || math.IsInf(f, 0) || f != math.Trunc(f) ||
+			f < 0 || f > math.MaxUint64 {
+			return 0, fmt.Errorf("msgpack: float %v cannot be represented as uint64", f)
+		}
+		return uint64(f), nil
 	}
 	return 0, fmt.Errorf("msgpack: invalid code=%x decoding uint64", c)
 }
@@ -158,6 +173,18 @@
 	case msgpcode.Uint64, msgpcode.Int64:
 		n, err := d.uint64()
 		return int64(n), err
+	case msgpcode.Float, msgpcode.Double:
+		// See uint(). Conversion succeeds only when the value is finite,
+		// integer-valued, and fits in int64.
+		f, err := d.float64(c)
+		if err != nil {
+			return 0, err
+		}
+		if math.IsNaN(f) || math.IsInf(f, 0) || f != math.Trunc(f) ||
+			f < math.MinInt64 || f > math.MaxInt64 {
+			return 0, fmt.Errorf("msgpack: float %v cannot be represented as int64", f)
+		}
+		return int64(f), nil
 	}
 	return 0, fmt.Errorf("msgpack: invalid code=%x decoding int64", c)
 }
